# Skill selection

`using-agent-skills` is the catalog. This rule only says how many skills to load. Do not copy that tree here.

## Budget

Load the smallest set that covers the task. Do not walk the full discovery tree. Do not read a SKILL.md unless that skill is in the set for this turn.

## Tiny work

Obvious one-file, low-risk edits: `git-workflow-and-versioning` only. Skip the rest; one line why if a lifecycle step is skipped.

## Non-trivial software change

These seven, in order (skip a step only with a one-line why):

1. `spec-driven-development`
2. `planning-and-task-breakdown`
3. `incremental-implementation`
4. `test-driven-development`
5. `debugging-and-error-recovery` — only when something is already broken
6. `code-review-and-quality`
7. `git-workflow-and-versioning`

Unclear intent before a spec: `interview-me` or `idea-refine`, then step 1.

## Extras — signal only

After the spine, add extras only for a matching branch of the `using-agent-skills` discovery tree (example: UI work → `frontend-ui-engineering`, not every Build skill).

If the job is not in that tree, match the task to a `description:` in `home/.cursor/skills/*/SKILL.md`. Do not open every file; search descriptions or use the name the user typed. User `/name` or an explicit skill name wins for this turn.

## Same job, two names

Prefer the core-lifecycle skill unless the user invoked the other:

| Job | Use | Not unless named |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TDD | `test-driven-development` | `tdd` |
| Review | `code-review-and-quality` | `code-review` |
| Debug | `debugging-and-error-recovery` | `diagnosing-bugs` |

## Slots the discovery tree does not have

- Hard-to-reverse stack, boundary, storage, or deploy: `architecture-decision-making` (then `documentation-and-adrs` only to record).
- GitHub issues, PRs, labels, merge: `github-workflow` (in addition to `git-workflow-and-versioning`).
- User looking for an installable skill: `find-skills`.

If an extra skill is used, one short note: which and why.
